ID: Ch8-Intro
TEXT:

 

"Barry!" Daren's voice cuts through the fog. "Hey, Barry. Can you hear me?"

A dull ache throbs behind my temples as the blur clears from my eyes. Cold flagstones press into my legs. We are sitting in a windowless stone cell, damp and choked with pale moss climbing the hairline fractures in the masonry. The only way out is a heavy iron grate set into the far archway, bolted shut from the other side. Ancient ruin work, by the cut of the blocks. Diane's lightning bolt flashes across my memory—the clearing, the sudden shock, all of us dropping into the grass. Did she drag us down here?

Daren stands over me, his plate armor clinking as he shifts his weight. Across the cell, Kate and Rose sit side by side against the opposite wall, knees drawn up against the chill. Hadrik looms near the iron bars, rapping his knuckles along the mortar to test the stone.

"What happened?" I ask, rubbing the back of my neck. "Did the lessathi drag us in?"

"Not the lessathi," Daren says. "The local wildlife. They took offense to us trampling through their sacred grove. I'll bring you up to speed on the rest, but first—can you PLEASE silence that infernal contraption? It's been chirping since I opened my eyes, and it's grinding on my last nerve."

I pull the stat device from my pocket. Four unallocated points glow on the display, charged by the residual magical currents from yesterday's battles.

The corridors around us hum with trapped mechanisms—I can hear springs coiled behind the walls, feel the floor tiles shift under uneven weight. Whatever built this gauntlet valued speed and instinct above all else. And deeper in, where the stonework turns to ancient lessathi script, I suspect only foresight will navigate what logic cannot.

choice("Invest points now", Ch8-Stats-spent, v_current_scene = Ch8-Stats-spent, special:stats)
choice("Invest points later(by pressing the stats button on the top of the screen)", Ch8-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch8-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: Ch8-Stats-spent
TEXT:

 

"Barry!" Daren's voice cuts through the fog. "Hey, Barry. Can you hear me?"

A dull ache throbs behind my temples as the blur clears from my eyes. Cold flagstones press into my legs. We are sitting in a windowless stone cell, damp and choked with pale moss climbing the hairline fractures in the masonry. The only way out is a heavy iron grate set into the far archway, bolted shut from the other side. Ancient ruin work, by the cut of the blocks. Diane's lightning bolt flashes across my memory—the clearing, the sudden shock, all of us dropping into the grass. Did she drag us down here?

Daren stands over me, his plate armor clinking as he shifts his weight. Across the cell, Kate and Rose sit side by side against the opposite wall, knees drawn up against the chill. Hadrik looms near the iron bars, rapping his knuckles along the mortar to test the stone.

"What happened?" I ask, rubbing the back of my neck. "Did the lessathi drag us in?"

"Not the lessathi," Daren says. "The local wildlife. They took offense to us trampling through their sacred grove. I'll bring you up to speed on the rest, but first—can you PLEASE silence that infernal contraption? It's been chirping since I opened my eyes, and it's grinding on my last nerve."

I pull the stat device from my pocket. Four unallocated points glow on the display, charged by the residual magical currents from yesterday's battles.

The corridors around us hum with trapped mechanisms—I can hear springs coiled behind the walls, feel the floor tiles shift under uneven weight. Whatever built this gauntlet valued speed and instinct above all else. And deeper in, where the stonework turns to ancient lessathi script, I suspect only foresight will navigate what logic cannot.

choice("Continue", Ch8-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch8-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: Ch8-Intro2
TEXT:

 I stow the stat device in my coat, and the shrill chime finally cuts out.

"Peace at last," Daren mutters. "Now maybe we can figure a way out of this pit without losing our minds."

"Hold on," I say, pushing myself up from the cold floor. "You said the animals brought us down here, but there isn't a guard in sight. Did anyone come by while I was out?"

"A tiger and a brown bear," Daren says. "Stood right outside the gate, growling low in their throats. I thought our jailers had sent their pets to intimidate us, but then Kate and Rose answered back in guttural clicks and chuffs. Turns out beasts and monsters across Varathia can speak—they just use ancient dialects. Monsters stick to ancient tongues because they know nothing else, and the animals are forbidden from speaking the common tongue by their spirit fox leader ever since human hunters began poaching them. Apparently, picked-up scraps of ancient speech aren't unusual for folks born on this continent."

"Never mind the linguistics," I say. "What do they want with us?"

"They told us to sit tight until their great golden fox shows up. Kept snickering about the lesson she's going to teach us."

"Then why leave the door unguarded? What stops us from smashing through the masonry and walking out?"

"These," Daren says, tapping a finger against his throat.

A dull green band clamps tight around his neck, etched with intricate, angular glyphs. I reach up and feel the same cold metal locked against my own pulse. I look over at Kate.

"Are these—"

"The same collars the ancients used at the Beacon of Hope," Kate cuts in, her voice flat and tight. "They suppress magical flow in artificial mages. The good news is I know how to disable them. The bad news is we have to reach the primary console powering the network first. These ruins are old lessathi architecture. If the layout matches the Beacon, the control room shouldn't be far."

"If the collars shut down magic," I say, looking at Hadrik, "why are you still a dwarf?"

"Puzzled me too," Hadrik says, running a thumb under the green band. "Doesn't dispel magic cast before the lock snapped shut—it just cuts off any new casting. First thing I tried was swelling back to my proper giant size, but the collar choked the shift before it could take."

"No magic," I say. "You still have your natural muscle, though?"

"Aye!" Hadrik grins, flexing his thick shoulders. "Can't dent the collar—these blasted things are tough as bedrock. But the walls? Plain mortar and weathered granite. I can punch right through them the second you give the word."

choice(""Perfect! Then let's not waste any more time here."", Ch8-Escape, v_current_scene = Ch8-Escape, v_ch8_talk = 0)
choice(""Wait, I think we should try to talk things through with their leader, first."", Ch8-Talk, v_current_scene = Ch8-Talk, v_ch8_talk = 1)


ID: Ch8-Talk
TEXT:

 "Wait, I think we should try to talk things through with their leader, first."

"Brilliant notion," Daren says, dripping sarcasm. "For all we know, this is a minor diplomatic misunderstanding and they only locked us in an underground dungeon to prepare the tea."

"Damn it, Daren, you know that's not what I—"

"Listen up, everyone!" Daren booms over me. "Time for a vote. Barry proposes we sit on our hands and wait for tea and honeycakes with the woodland gods. I propose we break out while the hallway is clear. All in favor of immediate escape, raise your hands."

Four hands go up at once. Daren and Hadrik beam in unison, Kate stares studiously at the floor, and Rose offers an apologetic cringe.

"Sorry, Barry," she says softly.

"Since we operate on majority rule," Daren says, smugness radiating from his grin, "you fell a few votes short of a victory, Barry. A real nail-biter. Hadrik, knock us an exit."

"Aye!" Hadrik winds up his fist.

"Hold on!" I snap. Hadrik stops mid-swing, glancing back over his broad shoulder.

"For the love of—what now?" Daren asks.

"Think for two seconds," I say. "Why isn't there a single guard at that grate? What if this is bait to catch us in a jailbreak and tack on a death sentence? Right now, our only crime is wandering onto their land. We're outsiders unfamiliar with the continent—there are extenuating circumstances. If we smash our way out, we'll run headfirst into beasts, we'll have to fight, and we might kill some of them. Do we really want an entire sovereign species hunting us across Varathia over a boundary dispute?"

Hadrik scratches at his thick black beard. "The lad speaks sense."

"You're not buying this!" Daren protests.

"Rose," I say, turning to her, "didn't you say their spirit fox is practically a deity? Unmatched power, thousands of years old?"

"She is," Rose says. "Ancient and immensely powerful. She could overwhelm most tournament mages without breaking a sweat."

"So would you rather make an enemy of a living god right out of the gate, when there's a chance to plead our case if we wait five minutes?"

Rose smiles faintly. "Put that way... I suppose I wouldn't."

choice("Continue", Ch8-Talk2, v_current_scene = Ch8-Talk2)


ID: Ch8-Talk2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch8_democratie,1)
achievement("Vive la democratie!",v_ac_ch8_democratie)
 "Come on!" Daren throws his hands up. "You all had your hands in the air a second ago!"

"What's wrong, Daren?" I smirk. "Losing faith in the democratic process?"

He scowls at me, his jaw tightening before he lets out a resigned breath.

"Fine," he grumbles. "The vote has turned. We'll hear out this grand golden fox and see where diplomacy lands us."

We don't have to wait long. Padded footsteps echo outside, and three figures halt before the iron bars.

At the center walks a fox whose coat shines like polished spun gold. The magical aura radiating from her is crushing—dense, ancient, and humming with an outworldly frequency that raises the hairs on my arms. It carries the distinct resonance of the magical plane, unmistakably close to the aura of a banshee.

Flanking her like retainers beside a noble are a spotted leopard and a massive black bull. The fox fixes her amber eyes on us and speaks in clear, unaccented common tongue.

"Greetings, outsiders," she says, her voice smooth and resonant. "I am Eleya, the golden fox. These are my lieutenants—Leo the leopard, and Taurus the bull."

"Leo and Taurus," Daren mutters under his breath. "Pinnacle of naming creativity."

"Isn't Leo supposed to be a lion?" Hadrik whispers back.

"Hush!" Rose whispers urgently, her eyes darting between the beasts.

The bull snorts, steam flaring from his nostrils as his dark eyes lock onto Daren with unmistakable hostility. The leopard remains motionless, gaze cool and detached. Eleya ignores the whisperers entirely, holding her head high.

"My subjects report you were apprehended trespassing upon our sacred grounds," she says. "What have you to say in your defense?"

choice(""We didn't know that we were on sacred grounds."", Ch8-Ignorance, v_current_scene = Ch8-Ignorance)
choice(""We meant to leave, but we were attacked and left there unconscious."", Ch8-Attacked, v_current_scene = Ch8-Attacked)


ID: Ch8-Ignorance
TEXT:

 "We didn't know that we were on sacred grounds."

"That is your defense?" Eleya's amber eyes narrow. "In what manner does ignorance pardon a violation? Do you deny setting foot upon our sanctum?"

"No, but—"

"Then I find you guilty as charged. I sentence you to a lifetime of hard labor within the animal kingdom. May the decades of toil grant you time to reflect upon your transgression."

"Hold on!" Daren steps toward the bars, armor clattering. "What kind of madness is this? A lifetime of servitude for taking a wrong turn in an unmarked forest? Do you execute your cubs and fawns if they step off a footpath?"

"You would do well to mind your tone, armored mage," Eleya says, cold and unyielding. "The animal kingdom maintains neither manicured paths nor decorative lawns. Yet if such a law existed and a cub broke it, I would order its execution without hesitation. Order is absolute. Your human leniency with law is precisely why your cities rot from within. I have heard enough. Your labor begins tomorrow at first light. Prepare yourselves."

She turns on her heel, striding away down the dark corridor. The bull gives Daren one last murderous glare and thuds after her.

Silence crashes down into the cell, heavy as a stone slab. A lifetime. Decades breaking rock in the dark under collar and claw. Daren stands motionless, his fists clenched tight at his sides. Kate stares blankly ahead, jaw locked, while Rose sinks against the masonry, pale and trembling.

Outside the grate, the leopard lingers.

Leo watches us in the dim torchlight, completely unhurried. The air near his paws ripples, and a sealed yellow envelope materializes out of empty space, settling softly onto the dust. He extends one spotted foreleg, claws clicking against the iron as he shoves the parchment beneath the bars and into our cell. His yellow eyes fix on mine—unblinking, unreadable—before he wheels around and pads into the shadows without a sound.

A cold prickle runs down my spine. An unprovoked clue from a beast lieutenant who despises humans? It stinks of a baited line.

Rose reaches out with a shaking hand, picks up the envelope, and breaks the seal. A slip of parchment unfolds in her fingers, covered in neat, common-tongue script.

"What is it?" Kate asks, stepping close.

Rose reads aloud, her voice barely a whisper. "'If you reach a corridor that splits into three paths, each marked with the symbol of an insect, choose the one marked with a beetle.'"

choice("Continue", Ch8-Escape, v_current_scene = Ch8-Escape)


ID: Ch8-Attacked
TEXT:

 "We meant to leave, but we were attacked and left there unconscious."

"You admit to waging battle upon our sanctum?" Eleya's voice turns razor-sharp. "That is a far graver offense than simple trespass. Bloodshed on sacred ground carries only one penalty: death."

"Wait—what?"

"You have until dawn to make peace with your gods and compose your final letters," she says evenly. "The executions will proceed at sunrise."

"Hold on!" Daren steps toward the bars, his armor rattling. "What kind of madness is this? An execution order because someone ambushed us in your woods? Do you slaughter your cubs and fawns if they step off a footpath?"

"You would do well to mind your tone, armored mage," Eleya says, cold and unyielding. "The animal kingdom maintains neither manicured paths nor decorative lawns. Yet if such a law existed and a cub broke it, I would order its execution without hesitation. Order is absolute. Your human leniency with law is precisely why your cities rot from within. I have heard enough. Use your remaining hours to meditate upon your end."

She turns on her heel, striding away down the dark corridor. The bull gives Daren one last murderous glare and thuds after her.

Silence crashes down into the cell, heavy as a stone slab. Execution at dawn. Less than a day left to live, stripped of magic, locked behind iron in an ancient tomb. Daren stares into the empty hallway, face drained of color. Kate's hands clench into white-knuckled fists, while Rose sinks against the stone, trembling violently.

Outside the grate, the leopard lingers.

Leo watches us in the dim torchlight, completely unhurried. The air near his paws ripples, and a sealed yellow envelope materializes out of empty space, settling softly onto the dust. He extends one spotted foreleg, claws clicking against the iron as he shoves the parchment beneath the bars and into our cell. His yellow eyes fix on mine—unblinking, unreadable—before he wheels around and pads into the shadows without a sound.

A cold prickle runs down my spine. An unprovoked escape route from a beast lieutenant who despises humans? It stinks of a baited trap.

Rose reaches out with a shaking hand, picks up the envelope, and breaks the seal. A slip of parchment unfolds in her fingers, covered in neat, common-tongue script.

"What is it?" Kate asks, stepping close.

Rose reads aloud, her voice barely a whisper. "'If you reach a corridor that splits into three paths, each marked with the symbol of an insect, choose the one marked with a beetle.'"

choice("Continue", Ch8-Escape, v_current_scene = Ch8-Escape)


ID: Ch8-Escape
TEXT:

#if(v_ch8_talk == 1) {
 "Hold on," Kate says, leaning over the parchment. "Does this mean the leopard expects us to break out, and he's leaving us directions?"

"So, Barry," Daren says, his voice carrying far louder than it needs to in the enclosed cell, "what do you propose we do next? Sit tight until sunrise to see if the golden fox softens her heart? Maybe we should hold another vote! What do you say, Barry? Care to call for another show of hands?"

The smug grin across his face is impossible to miss.

"That's enough, Daren," Kate cuts in. "None of us could have predicted the fox's verdict. They still left the corridor empty, so no ground was lost. If anything, we're better off than before, thanks to the note in Rose's hands."

"Aye!" Hadrik says. "We agreed to hear the fox out first. Worth a shot while it lasted."

With our sentencing confirmed, escape is our only path. We wait several minutes until the heavy tread of the beasts fades completely, and then Hadrik plants his feet and shatters the masonry with a single colossal punch.

We clamber over the cracked stones into the hall. The corridor stretches out in empty gloom, lit only by sputtering wall torches bracketed to the damp granite. From the dust and moss, the place could have sat abandoned for centuries. Our cell sits at the far dead end, leaving only one direction to move: left.

"I don't like this," Kate murmurs as we advance. "This is the same way the fox and her guard went. Running into them in these narrow halls is the last thing we need."

"Not like we were handed a map," Hadrik grunts. "One corridor, no forks. What boggles me is we haven't passed a single other cell. Isn't this supposed to be a dungeon? Where are the other convicts?"

"Perhaps they keep only one holding cell per wing," Rose suggests quietly.

"Forget other prisoners," I say, checking the shadowy corners ahead. "I'm worried about the lack of sentries. Did they honestly think stone walls could hold four mages and a warrior without wards?"

"Hey, Rose," Daren calls from a few paces ahead, peering at a carved panel in the stone. "Come take a look at this."

Rose hurries over to his shoulder. "What did you find?"

"You read ancient script, right? What does this say?"

Rose studies the carvings, her brows drawing together. "It's too intricate for me. I recognize the lessathi glyph style, but the dialect is far beyond my training."

"Barry," Daren says, glancing back at me. "Doesn't that gadget of yours have a setting for ancient languages? Step up and see what you make of it."

}
#if(v_ch8_talk == 0) {
 "Perfect! Then let's not waste any more time here."

"Stand back, then!" Hadrik says. "Here goes!"

The dwarf drives his fist into the wall with everything he has, blowing the granite blocks apart in a shower of stone and dust.

We clamber over the cracked rubble into the hall. The corridor stretches out in empty gloom, lit only by sputtering wall torches bracketed to the damp granite. From the dust and moss, the place could have sat abandoned for centuries. Our cell sits at the far dead end, leaving only one direction to move: left.

"I don't like this," Kate murmurs as we advance. "This is the path the tiger and bear took when they left to find their fox leader. Running into them in these narrow halls is the last thing we need."

"Not like we were handed a map," Hadrik grunts. "One corridor, no forks. What boggles me is we haven't passed a single other cell. Isn't this supposed to be a dungeon? Where are the other convicts?"

"Perhaps they keep only one holding cell per wing," Rose suggests quietly.

"Forget other prisoners," I say, checking the shadowy corners ahead. "I'm worried about the lack of sentries. Did they honestly think stone walls could hold four mages and a warrior without wards?"

"Hey, Rose," Daren calls from a few paces ahead, peering at a carved panel in the stone. "Come take a look at this."

Rose hurries over to his shoulder. "What did you find?"

"You read ancient script, right? What does this say?"

Rose studies the carvings, her brows drawing together. "It's too intricate for me. I recognize the lessathi glyph style, but the dialect is far beyond my training."

"Barry," Daren says, glancing back at me. "Doesn't that gadget of yours have a setting for ancient languages? Step up and see what you make of it."

}
choice("Continue", Ch8-Languages-failed, v_current_scene = Ch8-Languages-failed) if (v_ancient_languages < 3)
choice("Continue", Ch8-Languages-success, v_current_scene = Ch8-Languages-success) if (v_ancient_languages >= 3)


ID: Ch8-Languages-failed
TEXT:

 

I step up to the carved panel and trace the angular strokes, but the grammar is dense and the archaic radicals blur together. Without more points channeled into Ancient Languages, I can't extract more than scattered syllables.

"No good," I say. "My Ancient Languages stat isn't high enough to untangle the grammar."

"Then invest the points!" Daren says, throwing his hands wide. "Isn't that what the gadget is for?"

choice("Tell him that I don't have enough available points to level it up", Ch8-Available, v_current_scene = Ch8-Available, v_ch8_first_puzzle = 0)
choice("Tell him that my points are limited and that I'm saving them for later", Ch8-Limited, v_current_scene = Ch8-Limited, v_ch8_first_puzzle = 0)
choice("Decipher the glyphs (requires level 3 in ancient languages)", Ch8-Languages-success, v_current_scene = Ch8-Languages-success) if (v_ancient_languages >= 3)


ID: Ch8-Available
TEXT:

 "I don't have enough available points to bring my ancient languages stat to a high enough level."

"Seriously?" Daren groans. "You burn through points the instant you earn them? Did the concept of an emergency reserve never cross your mind?"

He lets out a heavy breath.

"Standing here gawk-eyed won't get us out," he says, turning back down the hall. "We keep moving and—"

He cuts off, freezing mid-stride.

Blocking the corridor is a barefoot girl barely four feet tall, standing with her hands clasped behind her back and a bright grin on her face. She wears voluminous, colorful circus-style trousers, a simple gray tank top, and a polished golden amulet resting on her chest. Her bright red hair cascades nearly to her waist. She looks nine or ten at most, yet no protective ward triggers in my mind. She isn't registered as a local civilian.

"Hello!" she chirps, tilting her head.

"Where in the gods' names did you drop from?" Daren asks, his hand instinctively twitching toward his hilt.

"There!" She points a finger straight up at a ragged hole in the stone ceiling.

"Right," Daren says, blinking. "And what are you doing in an abandoned dungeon?"

"We were training in the chamber above when we heard voices down here. I'm Flower. Pleased to meet you!"

"Hold on," Daren says, holding up a palm. "Who is 'we'?"

Flower stiffens, her cheerful smile twitching.

"Did I say 'we'?" Her laugh is quick and nervous. "I meant 'I.' I was training upstairs. No 'we.' Just me. Just I."

choice("Continue", Ch8-Training, v_current_scene = Ch8-Training)


ID: Ch8-Limited
TEXT:

 "I'm not going to spend my points just to translate some obscure writing on a wall. There are other stats that are far more important than ancient languages."

"Fine, fine. Forget I asked."

Daren lets out a heavy breath.

"Standing here gawk-eyed won't get us out," he says, turning back down the hall. "We keep moving and—"

He cuts off, freezing mid-stride.

