choice("Continue", Ch8-Purification, v_current_scene = Ch8-Purification)


ID: Ch8-Purification
TEXT:

 "W-what are you doing?" Flower shrinks back as Daren draws his broadsword and begins weaving the blade through formal ritual arcs in the air around her.

"Don't worry, Flower," I say calmly. "His ritual won't take hold. Neither on you nor Petal."

"If... if you say so," she murmurs, tracking the gleaming steel with wide eyes.

Daren completes the somatic sequences, holding the sword aloft to draw down the cleansing resonance. For several long seconds, nothing happens. The runes along the flat remain dead and dull. The white glow refuses to spark.

"It... it isn't taking," Daren mutters, his arm dropping slowly.

"Because the technique only binds a dormant banshee before awakening, or disperses false auras," I say. "An awakened entity's spiritual frequency is too volatile for a purification weave to anchor. To sever Petal now, you would have to strike down the girl's physical body. Is that your vow, Daren?"

"No," Daren says, his voice faltering as he lowers the blade entirely. "I would never..."

"Listen to me!" Flower stamps a bare foot on the stone. "Petal is staying, whether you approve or not! You don't have to speak to her! And I'm still here! I might not be the most reliable guide in Varathia, but I'm the only one you have! I promised to lead you to the control room, and I keep my promises. Are you coming or not?"

choice(""Sure. It's not like we have a better alternative at this point."", Ch8-Alternative, v_current_scene = Ch8-Alternative)
choice(""Please, tell me that you at least have a vague idea of where we are headed."", Ch8-Vague, v_current_scene = Ch8-Vague)


ID: Ch8-Alternative
TEXT:

 "Sure. It's not like we have a better alternative at this point."

"Agreed," Kate says. "A flawed guide is still better than blind exploration in hostile ruins."

Hadrik rolls his shoulders. "Count me in. Last time I took the lead, we ended up in a hydra's lap. I forfeit my right to complain."

"I..." Rose's voice is still quiet, her color slowly returning. "I think we should follow Flower as well."

"Right, then!" Flower beams, her posture snapping straight. "You won't regret it! Follow my lead!"

She sets off at a brisk, exaggerated march, chanting under her breath: "Hut, two, three, four."

"I am regretting this already," Daren mutters under his breath as we fall in behind her.

A short walk through winding masonry brings us to a vertical stone shaft fitted with a platform and iron counterweights. Several iron levers jut from the side console.

"This lift takes us straight back to the upper hall, past all the traps!" Flower announces as we crowd onto the platform. "Now... which lever was the ascent again?"

She stares at the row of handles, her brows pinching together.

"Ooh, they all look identical!" she groans after ten seconds of silent scrutiny.

A dry rattle issues from the golden amulet at her chest.

"What is it, Arraka?" Flower opens the locket.

"Second from the right, little fool!" the discordant chorus sings out. "Pulls you right up to the gallery! I swear it on my mother!"

Flower narrows her eyes. "You aren't lying to me again?"

"Perish the thought! Sacred oath!"

"All right, then," Flower says, hauling the lever back.

Gears clank, cables groan, and the elevator plunges downward into the dark.

choice("Continue", Ch8-Arraka, v_current_scene = Ch8-Arraka)


ID: Ch8-Vague
TEXT:

 "Please, tell me that you at least have a vague idea of where we are headed."

"Of course I do!" Flower says. "We need to return to the upper hall. There's a lift nearby that bypasses the trap corridor entirely and drops us right at the control sector entrance!"

"Sounds sensible enough," Hadrik grunts.

"I agree," Kate says. "We have no time to wander blindly while the spirit fox hunts us. We follow the girl."

"I..." Rose's voice is still quiet, her color slowly returning. "I think we should follow Flower as well."

"Right, then!" Flower beams, her posture snapping straight. "You won't regret it! Follow my lead!"

She sets off at a brisk, exaggerated march, chanting under her breath: "Hut, two, three, four."

"I am regretting this already," Daren mutters under his breath as we fall in behind her.

A short walk through winding masonry brings us to a vertical stone shaft fitted with a platform and iron counterweights. Several iron levers jut from the side console.

"Here it is!" Flower announces as we crowd onto the platform. "Now... which lever was the ascent again?"

She stares at the row of handles, her brows pinching together.

"Ooh, they all look identical!" she groans after ten seconds of silent scrutiny.

A dry rattle issues from the golden amulet at her chest.

"What is it, Arraka?" Flower opens the locket.

"Second from the right, little fool!" the discordant chorus sings out. "Pulls you right up to the gallery! I swear it on my mother!"

Flower narrows her eyes. "You aren't lying to me again?"

"Perish the thought! Sacred oath!"

"All right, then," Flower says, hauling the lever back.

Gears clank, cables groan, and the elevator plunges downward into the dark.

choice("Continue", Ch8-Arraka, v_current_scene = Ch8-Arraka)


ID: Ch8-Arraka
TEXT:

 "You swore!" Flower shouts at the amulet. "You swore on your mother!"

"I don't have a mother, you idiot!" Arraka shrieks, dissolving into hysterical laughter.

The stone platform rumbles downward into the depths. Guttural growls and wet skittering echo from the black pits below.

"That is quite enough," Flower says, her voice turning frigid as her eyes flash bright sapphire blue.

She steps to the control console, hands moving with practiced precision over the levers. The descent shudders to a halt, gears reversing as the platform begins ascending smoothly toward the upper level.

"Ruining the fun as always, aren't you, Illuna?" the amulet rasps as the girl's eyes shift back to emerald green.

"Illuna?" I ask. "Is that the banshee's real name?"

"Never mind that," Hadrik says, leaning down toward the pendant. "Who is the loudmouth in the locket?"

"Who, me?" Arraka coos. "Why, I am the spirit of boundless joy and merriment! This cruel child keeps me locked in here, preventing me from showering the world in gladness! Won't you rescue me from this wicked captor?"

"Spirit of joy?" Hadrik chuckles, arms crossed over his chest. "I take it that suffocating, bloodthirsty aura boiling off your vessel is just decorative, then?"

"Oh?" Arraka chirps. "Pardon me! I forgot to adjust the setting!"

The oppressive, suffocating density around the amulet vanishes instantly, replaced by a radiant, warm aura that feels like springtime sunshine.

"See? The very embodiment of cheer!" Arraka purrs in the high, melodic voice of a polite schoolgirl. "Ask anyone who has had the privilege of crossing my path! I always leave them smiling! Granted, confirming that might prove difficult since every last one is rotting in the ground. And on second thought, their final expressions tended toward raw, soul-shredding agony. But I distinctly recall carving wide grins into a few dozen throats, so surely that counts! A-ha-ha—AHAHAHA—"

Flower snaps the amulet shut, squeezing the golden casing until her knuckles turn white.

"Another banshee?" I ask as the elevator clunks into place at the upper corridor.

"An ancient one," Flower mutters, tucking the pendant beneath her tank top. "The lessathi trapped her in this vessel twenty years ago. We've been her wardens ever since."

choice(""Why? Did the lessathi ask you to?"", Ch8-Lessathi, v_current_scene = Ch8-Lessathi, v_ch8_old = 0)
choice(""Wait, did you just say twenty years ago? How old are you, exactly?"", Ch8-Old, v_current_scene = Ch8-Old, v_ch8_old = 1)


ID: Ch8-Lessathi
TEXT:

 "Why? Did the lessathi ask you to?"

"What?" Flower looks appalled. "No! Who would work for those tyrants? We keep her contained because if she breaks out, she'll slaughter half the continent! Come on, the button room is just ahead!"

"Isn't she more trouble than she's worth?" I ask as we follow her down the stone passage. "Why not bury the vessel at the bottom of a lake?"

"She isn't all bad!" Flower insists. "She's helped us plenty of times, and saved our necks more than once. Besides, Petal keeps her in check, and—"

She skids to a halt, eyes wide. "Look!"

"What is it?" Kate asks.

"The three branch corridors!" Flower points ahead. "One leads to the control room, and the other two trigger lethal defenses. I know for a fact the safe path is marked with an insect glyph! We're practically there!"

"An insect?" Kate murmurs, as Flower darts ahead to examine the archways.

choice("Continue", Ch8-Corridors, v_current_scene = Ch8-Corridors)


ID: Ch8-Old
TEXT:

 "Wait, did you just say twenty years ago? How old are you, exactly?"

"Turning thirty next month!" Flower beams over her shoulder. "Look, we've reached the junction! Follow me, the button room is right ahead!"

"You... don't look thirty," I note as we hurry after her.

"My physical growth froze twenty years ago when Petal and I fused! Petal keeps nagging that I technically never passed nine, but I refuse to miss my birthdays! If I want my cake—"

She skids to a halt, eyes wide. "Look!"

"What is it?" Kate asks.

"The three branch corridors!" Flower points ahead. "One leads to the control room, and the other two trigger lethal defenses. I know for a fact the safe path is marked with an insect glyph! We're practically there!"

"An insect?" Kate murmurs, as Flower darts ahead to examine the archways.

choice("Continue", Ch8-Corridors, v_current_scene = Ch8-Corridors)


ID: Ch8-Corridors
TEXT:

#if(v_ch8_talk == 0) {
 "No, no, no!" Flower wails as we reach the junction where the path divides in three.

Above each stone archway, an insect glyph is carved deep into the keystone. She was right about the insect symbol, but failed to mention that all three passages bear one.

"A dragonfly, a beetle, and a wasp," Kate observes, scanning the lintels. "Flower, you're certain one of these routes leads straight to the control hub?"

"Yes! The safe path goes directly to the console room without a single defense mechanism," Flower groans, clutching her head. "I just can't remember which bug is the right one!"

The three archways yawn before us, identical save for the glyphs. No visual tells. No scuff marks or temperature differences. If the wrong corridor seals behind us and triggers a floor-collapse trap, there will be no time to react—only foreknowledge would save us from a drop we cannot outrun.

}
#if(v_ch8_talk == 1) {
 "No, no, no!" Flower wails as we reach the junction where the path divides in three.

Above each stone archway, an insect glyph is carved deep into the keystone. She was right about the insect symbol, but failed to mention that all three passages bear one.

"Wait," Kate says, stepping toward the center arch. "The leopard's note mentioned three insect corridors. A dragonfly, a beetle, and a wasp. Leo told us to take the beetle."

"Leo?" Flower blinks. "Leo the leopard?"

"You know him?" Kate asks.

"Of course I know him! But why would he help you? He despises humans..."

"You think it's an ambush?" Kate asks.

"I don't know!" Flower groans, clutching her head. "I just can't remember which bug is the right one!"

The three archways yawn before us, identical save for the glyphs. No visual tells. No scuff marks or temperature differences. If the wrong corridor seals behind us and triggers a floor-collapse trap, there will be no time to react—only foreknowledge would save us from a drop we cannot outrun.

}
choice("Suggest taking the path marked with a dragonfly", Ch8-Dragonfly, v_current_scene = Ch8-Dragonfly)
choice("Suggest taking the path marked with a beetle", Ch8-Beetle, v_current_scene = Ch8-Beetle)
choice("Suggest taking the path marked with a wasp", Ch8-Wasp, v_current_scene = Ch8-Wasp)


ID: Ch8-Dragonfly
TEXT:

#if(v_ch8_talk == 0) {
 "If we know that at least one of these paths is the correct one, then we may as well just pick one at random and if we trigger any traps, we get out of there as soon as possible. I say we try the corridor marked with a dragonfly first."

"One path is as good as another," Daren says. "Though I'll gladly endorse Barry's pick, so when we step on a pressure plate, I know exactly who to hold responsible."

With the party content to let me carry the blame, we head down the dragonfly corridor.

Five paces past the threshold, a heavy stone portcullis drops behind us with a thunderous crash, sealing the entrance. Above, the ceiling groans and begins a slow, steady descent.

"Oh no..." Flower gasps, slapping her cheeks. "Now I remember! It was the beetle! The beetle was the safe route!"

"Hardly a disaster," Hadrik grunts. "The roof is barely creeping. I'll punch the rear wall out and we'll head back."

"Don't!" Flower shouts. "The rear slab is rigged with spring-loaded poison darts!"

Hadrik scratches his beard. "The beetle hall runs directly to our left. If I punch through the partition wall, we step right into the middle corridor." He glances around. "Assuming no one objects."

"Partition walls don't carry trap mechanisms," Flower confirms.

"Knock it down, Hadrik," Kate says.

Hadrik grins, driving his fist through the masonry in an explosion of mortar.

We clamber through the breach into the beetle passage. Ahead, a steady amber glow washes across the stone floor.

"Light ahead!" Flower points toward the exit, bouncing on her heels. "The console room is right through that archway! Forward march!"

}
#if(v_ch8_talk == 1) {
 "To me, it sounds like the leopard is trying to lure us into a trap," I say. "At least that only leaves us with two other corridors to choose from. I say we just go with the dragonfly corridor, and if we trigger any traps, we get out of there as soon as possible."

"We're gambling on incomplete information either way," Daren says. "I'll back Barry's guess—at least that gives me someone to blame if we trigger a spike pit."

With the party content to let me carry the blame, we head down the dragonfly corridor.

Five paces past the threshold, a heavy stone portcullis drops behind us with a thunderous crash, sealing the entrance. Above, the ceiling groans and begins a slow, steady descent.

"Oh no..." Flower gasps, slapping her cheeks. "Leo wasn't lying! It was the beetle all along!"

"Hardly a disaster," Hadrik grunts. "The roof is barely creeping. I'll punch the rear wall out and we'll head back."

"Don't!" Flower shouts. "The rear slab is rigged with spring-loaded poison darts!"

Hadrik scratches his beard. "The beetle hall runs directly to our left. If I punch through the partition wall, we step right into the middle corridor." He glances around. "Assuming no one objects."

"Partition walls don't carry trap mechanisms," Flower confirms.

"Knock it down, Hadrik," Kate says.

Hadrik grins, driving his fist through the masonry in an explosion of mortar.

We clamber through the breach into the beetle passage. Ahead, a steady amber glow washes across the stone floor.

"Light ahead!" Flower points toward the exit, bouncing on her heels. "The console room is right through that archway! Forward march!"

}
choice("Continue", Ch8-Control-room, v_current_scene = Ch8-Control-room)


ID: Ch8-Wasp
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_ch8_talk == 0 && v_premonition < 4 || v_ch8_talk == 1 && v_premonition < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_ch8_talk == 0 && v_premonition == 4 || v_ch8_talk == 1 && v_premonition == 4)
#if(v_ch8_talk == 0 && v_premonition < 4) {
 

"If we know that at least one of these paths is the correct one, then we may as well just pick one at random and if we trigger any traps, we get out of there as soon as possible. I say we try the corridor marked with a wasp first."

"One path is as good as another," Daren says. "Though I'll gladly endorse Barry's pick, so when we step on a pressure plate, I know exactly who to hold responsible."

With everyone agreeing to let me carry the blame, we step under the wasp archway.

A stone slab drops behind us with a deafening slam, sealing the corridor.

We freeze, scanning the walls for dart slits or pendulum grooves.

"Perhaps this wing has no defenses?" Hadrik muses.

The flagstones shear away beneath our boots. The entire floor gives out into a sheer pit lined with rows of upright iron spikes. Pain flares across my chest as five iron tips drive through my torso. Cold darkness closes in before I can draw another breath.

}
#if(v_ch8_talk == 1 && v_premonition < 4) {
 

"To me, it sounds like the leopard is trying to lure us into a trap," I say. "At least that only leaves us with two other corridors to choose from. I say we just go with the wasp corridor, and if we trigger any traps, we get out of there as soon as possible."

"We're gambling on incomplete information either way," Daren says. "I'll back Barry's guess—at least that gives me someone to blame if we trigger a spike pit."

With everyone agreeing to let me carry the blame, we step under the wasp archway.

A stone slab drops behind us with a deafening slam, sealing the corridor.

We freeze, scanning the walls for dart slits or pendulum grooves.

"Perhaps this wing has no defenses?" Hadrik muses.

The flagstones shear away beneath our boots. The entire floor gives out into a sheer pit lined with rows of upright iron spikes. Pain flares across my chest as five iron tips drive through my torso. Cold darkness closes in before I can draw another breath.

}
#if(v_ch8_talk == 0 && v_premonition == 4) {
 

"If we know that at least one of these paths is the correct one, then we may as well just pick one at random and if we trigger any traps, we get out of there as soon as possible. I say we try the corridor marked with a wasp first."

"One path is as good as another," Daren says. "Though I'll gladly endorse Barry's pick, so when we step on a pressure plate, I know exactly who to hold responsible."

With everyone agreeing to let me carry the blame, we step under the wasp archway.

A stone slab crashes down behind us, sealing the entrance.

A blinding premonition flashes through my mind: the center floor collapsing into a pit of jagged iron spikes, dragging all six of us into the dark.

"To the walls! Now!" I yell. "The center floor is collapsing! Press against the masonry and don't move!"

"What?" Flower blinks.

Daren, Kate, Hadrik, and Rose flatten themselves against the outer wall instantly.

The center flagstones give way with a sickening crack, dropping into the abyss. Flower plunges into the pit, but jets of roaring flame flare from her bare feet, launching her straight back up to hover level with the ledge.

"That was amazing, Barry!" Flower calls down, drifting on two steady fire jets. "Can you see the future?"

"Roughly," I say, steadying my breath against the wall. "A premonition subroutine in the device."

"Fascinating!" she beams, then gasps, covering her mouth with both hands. "The beetle! The beetle was the safe corridor! Why didn't I remember earlier?"

"We survived in one piece thanks to the lad's gadget," Hadrik says. "I'll punch through the dividing wall to our right and put us in the middle passage."

"No traps in the partition," Flower calls down.

"Do it, Hadrik," Kate says.

Hadrik punches through the stone with a thunderous blow. We shimmy along the ledge, clamber through the breach, and enter the beetle passage.

Ahead, light washes across the floor from the far archway.

"That's the exit!" Flower cheers, touching down to run ahead. "Control room right ahead! Follow me!"

}
