ID: Ch8-Beetle
TEXT:

#if(v_ch8_talk == 0) {
 "If we know that at least one of these paths is the correct one, then we may as well just pick one at random and if we trigger any traps, we get out of there as soon as possible. I say we try the corridor marked with a beetle first."

"One path is as good as another," Daren says. "Though I'll gladly endorse Barry's pick, so when we step on a pressure plate, I know exactly who to hold responsible."

With the party content to let me carry the blame, we advance down the beetle corridor.

The passage remains still. No grinding mechanisms, no falling portcullises. After a few minutes of brisk walking, warm light washes over the masonry ahead.

"There it is!" Flower points toward the archway, practically skipping. "No traps, just like I promised! The control room is right through here! Come on!"

}
#if(v_ch8_talk == 1) {
 "I don't think the leopard would have chosen such a roundabout way of having us killed when he could have simply told the fox that we were planning to escape, and have her execute us instead. I say we go with the corridor marked with a beetle, and if we trigger any traps, we get out of there as soon as possible."

"We're gambling on incomplete information either way," Daren says. "I'll back Barry's instincts—if he's wrong, at least the blame is neatly settled."

With everyone in agreement, we advance down the beetle corridor.

The passage remains still. No grinding mechanisms, no falling portcullises. After a few minutes of brisk walking, warm light washes over the masonry ahead.

"There it is!" Flower points toward the archway, practically skipping. "No traps, just like I promised! The control room is right through here! Come on!"

}
choice("Continue", Ch8-Control-room, v_current_scene = Ch8-Control-room)


ID: Ch8-Control-room
TEXT:

#if(v_ch8_talk == 0) {
 Flower bounds through the archway, but her momentum dies the moment she crosses the threshold. She freezes in the center of the chamber.

As I step into the room behind her, my eyes take a moment to adjust to the intense golden glare radiating from the far dais.

"Congratulations," a smooth, resonant voice echoes across the vaulted stone. "You have reached the primary control hub. Now you may die with the satisfaction of having achieved your objective."

Eleya stands atop the central dais, her golden coat glowing with blinding brilliance. Her magical aura rolls over the room in suffocating waves—ancient, immense, and vibrating with outworldly density that rivals a banshee's presence. Beside her stands Leo the leopard, watching us in silence.

The chamber around them is packed with brass dials, glowing rune plates, and banks of levers, the walls etched with intricate lessathi diagrams.

"The moment your breakout was reported," Eleya says, "I knew your destination. Chasing you through tunnels would have been an inefficient use of my time, so I awaited you here. A foolish choice to flee your cell. Had you waited patiently, your sentence would have been mere labor in our realm. But fleeing confinement before judgment is an unforgivable defiance. It carries the penalty of death."

"Are you serious?" Daren shouts, stepping forward. "A death sentence for walking out of an unlocked ruin? Do you butcher your cubs and fawns if they step off a trail?"

"Mind your tongue, armored mage," Eleya says, her gaze freezing him in place. "Order is absolute. Your human leniency is why your civilization fractures. I will hear no further protests."

Golden light flares around us. Bars of solid magical energy erupt from the flagstones, snapping shut in an impenetrable cage that encloses the five of us, leaving only Flower outside.

"Your existence is no longer worth another second of my time," Eleya says, stepping forward. "I will execute you here and now."

The raw pressure radiating from her presses down like a mountain of lead. It exceeds even the heavy presence of Azarius, carrying the same hopeless, insurmountable dread as our encounter with Eiden. Stripped of magic, we cannot mount even a fraction of resistance.

"Eleya, stop!" Flower throws herself between the golden fox and our cage, arms spread wide. "You can't kill them! They're good people!"

"Step aside, Flower," Eleya commands. "They violated our laws. They must be destroyed."

"Can't you just give them a warning or a chore?"

"I will not entertain your interference, child. You have assisted us in the past, but if you insist on shielding these trespassers, you will share their fate."

"You are always so stubborn!" Flower's voice sharpens as her eyes blaze a fiery, radiant crimson. A scorching heat washes from her small frame, the air rippling with power. "Fine! If you want a fight, you get one! Petal, ready!"

}
